          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-victoria-4f6c4f26ac0c6d1e\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Victoria directly and emphatically avows that she loves Johannes, explicitly affirming the truth and considered sincerity of that state. The utterance does not undertake a relationship; instead, she immediately declares such a relationship impossible. Her claim to have reflected night and day weighs strongly against construing the words as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Victoria directly addresses Johannes in a private face-to-face conversation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Victoria uses the avowal to resolve Johannes’s uncertainty and deny that her earlier declaration was a lie. Her love is presented as genuine but unable to become a relationship because of social and familial barriers, especially her expectation that her father would refuse Johannes. The secrecy and urgency of the meeting intensify the scene without changing the core force from a considered truth-conditional avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg elsker Dem” (“I love you”).\",\"supports\":\"A direct present-tense avowal of Victoria’s loving emotional state toward Johannes, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg løi ikke iforgårs og lyver ikke nu” (“I did not lie the day before yesterday and am not lying now”).\",\"supports\":\"Victoria explicitly asserts the truth and continuity of her declaration, reinforcing truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg har tænkt på det nat og dag; jeg mener hvad jeg har sagt. Men det blir umulig.” (“I have thought about it night and day; I mean what I have said. But it will be impossible.”)\",\"supports\":\"Deliberate reflection supports a considered avowal rather than reflexive discharge, while the declaration of impossibility argues against relational undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Victoria explains that her father would refuse Johannes and asks why he forces her to say it.\",\"supports\":\"The obstacle concerns whether love can become a socially recognized relationship; it does not undermine the avowed emotional state or create performative commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the considered avowal. P is unnecessary because Victoria undertakes no commitment and expressly rejects the relationship as impossible; E is unnecessary because emotional intensity and urgency do not outweigh the explicit framing as reflective, truth-asserting speech. No important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
